From 5c709c7a50d69dd7975e3ddd7b7acacf72f6402b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(hud): render native locator groups

Render the core locator-group snapshot with accepted Bedrock textures,
ARGB tint, actor binding, and distance-dependent position smoothing.
Keep invisible groups distinct from an empty stream, preserve default
Java waypoint behavior until a native snapshot arrives, and reject queued
payloads from a disconnected session.

Native reference: Bedrock 1.26.51.1, build 51061372, protocol 2193.
Controlled captures and native draw routine 0x148dc8e60 establish the
182-by-5 bar, strict 60-degree bearing range, size*5+2 icon extent,
FOV-dependent height arrows, fixed arrow geometry, overlap order, and
20-to-2 smoothing rates. Zero and negative size values remain valid.
Native resource initialization is at 0x148dcb400. Assets and captures stay
private; production resolves textures through accepted resource packs.

This implements the default native locator renderer. Full resource-pack
HUD definition overrides, legacy player-location presentation, and the
remaining native comparison matrix are still incomplete.

Use native float normalization and the camera forward vector for horizontal
bearing. Keep the native atan2(z,x) zero-vector result and signed wrapping.
Deriving bearing from yaw and unnormalized doubles changed visibility for
vertical and near-coincident targets and at a rounded 60-degree boundary.

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 draw 0x145745690, instructions 0x145746a56..0x145746b64,
establish normalization, the 0.0001 cutoff and the visibility branch.
Fifty-five original-instruction controls with supplied cardinal camera
vectors and named atan2f/fmodf import boundaries expose nine old visibility
differences and agree after the change. Three behavior regressions fail
before and pass after. These controls do not establish complete camera,
texture, vertical-arrow or installed HUD parity. The reported missing
locator bar on its original server remains unmatched.

Serve converted UI images that the converter shared with an identical
image. When no pack provides a requested server UI image, the namespace
resource manager reads the alias table and returns the shared bytes from
the pack that wrote the table, under the requested identifier. Textures
therefore load through their normal UI identifier, a direct override of
that identifier still wins, and overriding the unrelated shared target
cannot change UI pixels. Each resource manager generation owns its table
cache, so a reload discards it with the old packs.
